        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "Terai makes the violin a first-line jazz voice rather than ornamental color. In this quartet, bow attack, long melodic lines and rhythmic phrasing sit directly against Kitajima's piano and a compact bass-drums engine, so the attraction is the changing handoff of melody, harmony and pulse in the room. It is a clear Listening Notes fit because the instrument itself carries a distinct Japanese jazz identity without needing an anniversary or celebrity frame to justify admission.",
